---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Treadmill Workout for Seniors: A 6-Week Walking Plan";
const description =
  "A gentle six-week treadmill walking plan for older adults: minutes, speed, incline and effort by week, three sample sessions and safe balance add-ons.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Workouts & Training", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/training/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Workout for Seniors", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-workout-for-seniors/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good treadmill workout for seniors?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most older adults, a good workout is a few minutes of slow warm-up walking, 10 to 30 minutes at an effort where you can still talk in full sentences, and a few minutes of slow cool-down, three to five days a week. Start flat and slow, often 1.0 to 2.0 mph, and build time before adding a gentle incline of 1 to 3% or a little speed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long should a 70-year-old walk on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Begin with about 10 minutes of main walking plus warm-up and cool-down, and build toward 20 to 30 minutes over several weeks. Four or five 30-minute walks add up to the 150 minutes of moderate activity a week the US guidelines recommend, but any amount helps, and two 10-minute walks are a reasonable substitute for one 20-minute walk.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What speed should a senior walk on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "There is no single right speed. Many older adults start between 1.0 and 2.0 mph and settle somewhere around 1.5 to 3.0 mph for their main walk. Use the talk test: for most of the session you should be breathing a little harder but still able to talk in full sentences. Change speed in 0.1 to 0.2 mph steps.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should seniors use incline on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A gentle incline of 1 to 3% is a good way to add effort without walking faster, which many older walkers find steadier. Build up to it after two or three weeks of flat walking, use it on one or two sessions a week, and check with a physical therapist first if you have knee or hip arthritis or a recent joint replacement.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it OK for seniors to hold the treadmill handrails?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Light contact for balance is what the rails are for, and for some people it is the right long-term choice. What to avoid is gripping hard to keep up with the belt or leaning on the rails, which means the speed or incline is too high. Slow down until a light touch is enough.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many days a week should seniors do treadmill workouts?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three days a week is a good start, with a rest day between sessions. Build to four or five days as the walks feel comfortable, and add balance and strength exercises on two days a week. Rest or go back a week if you feel unusually tired or have joint pain that lasts into the next day.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-17",
      dateModified: "2026-11-17",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/treadmill-workout-for-seniors/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "training");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const GROUP_PAGES = GROUP?.pages ?? [];
const HERE_AT = GROUP_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.url === HERE);
// The group's first 2 pages (its leads), then the pages after this one, wrapping round, so every page in a long
// hub group gets sibling links and the leads keep theirs (PLAYBOOK §0b L1).
const MORE = [...new Set([...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, 2), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(HERE_AT + 1), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, Math.max(HERE_AT, 0))])].filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-17",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-17",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill workout for seniors",
      "treadmill plan for older adults",
      "senior walking plan",
      "beginner treadmill workout",
      "balance exercises for seniors",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the 6-Week Plan", link: "#the-plan" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/training/">Workouts & Training</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Training</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill Workout for Seniors</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A six-week walking plan written for older adults: short, steady sessions that build time first, then a little incline, then a little speed. Every week has its minutes, speed range, grade and target effort, plus simple balance and strength work done safely beside the machine.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 17, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on published exercise guidance. General information, not medical advice.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#the-plan" class="btn-ghost">The 6-Week Plan</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Most treadmill plans are written for people in their thirties who want to run a 5K. If you
          are in your sixties, seventies or eighties, the aims are different: walk comfortably for
          longer, feel steadier on your feet, keep your heart and legs working, and do it without a
          sore knee or a scare on the belt. This plan is built around those aims. It starts slowly,
          changes one thing at a time, and tells you plainly when to hold back.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#before-you-start">Before You Start: Doctor, Shoes and the Machine</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-hard">How Hard Should It Feel?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warm-up-cool-down">Warm-Up and Cool-Down</a></li>
            <li><a href="#the-plan">The 6-Week Plan</a></li>
            <li><a href="#sample-sessions">Three Sample Sessions</a></li>
            <li><a href="#balance-strength">Balance and Strength Add-Ons, Beside the Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-to-progress">When to Progress</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-to-back-off">When to Back Off, and When to Stop</a></li>
            <li><a href="#the-right-machine">Does Your Treadmill Suit the Plan?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Verdict</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A good treadmill workout for seniors is 10 to 30 minutes of walking at an
            effort where you can still talk in full sentences, three to five days a week, with a
            few minutes of slow walking at each end.</strong> Start on a flat belt at a speed that
            feels almost too easy, often somewhere between 1.0 and 2.0 mph, and build time before
            you add any incline or speed.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Weeks 1 and 2:</strong> 10 to 15 minutes, flat, easy effort. The goal is
            to get used to the moving belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Weeks 3 and 4:</strong> 15 to 25 minutes, a gentle 1 to 3% incline on some
            days, moderate effort.</li>
            <li><strong>Weeks 5 and 6:</strong> 20 to 30 minutes, with one short-interval session
            a week if everything has felt good.</li>
            <li><strong>Twice a week:</strong> five to ten minutes of balance and leg-strength
            work next to the treadmill, with the belt stopped.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Check with your doctor first if you have a heart or lung condition, diabetes, balance
            problems, a history of falls, or a recent joint replacement. Clip the safety key to
            your clothing every time.
          </p>
          <p>
            This page is a plan for older walkers specifically. If you are younger and simply new
            to the machine, our general <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-beginners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workout for beginners</a> moves faster, and once this plan feels easy, the sessions in <a href="/treadmill-walking-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking workouts</a> are the natural next step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-you-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Before You Start: Doctor, Shoes and the Machine</h2>
<p>
            A few minutes of preparation removes most of the risk from treadmill walking. None of
            it is complicated, and all of it is worth doing before session one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Talk to your doctor if any of these apply.</strong> A heart condition or chest
            pain on exertion, high blood pressure that is not well controlled, lung disease,
            diabetes (especially with numb feet), dizziness or fainting spells, a fall in the past
            year, Parkinson's disease or another condition that affects balance, osteoporosis, or
            a hip or knee replacement in the past few months. Ask two specific questions: is
            moderate walking safe for me, and is there anything I should avoid, such as incline or
            certain heart-rate levels? If you take a beta-blocker, your heart rate will not rise
            normally with effort, which is one reason this plan uses the talk test rather than
            heart-rate numbers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wear proper shoes.</strong> Closed walking or <a href="/best-running-shoes-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>running shoes</a> with a firm heel
            and a sole that grips. Not slippers, not socks, not sandals. Loose laces are a
            surprisingly common hazard on a moving belt, so double-knot them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Learn the machine with the belt stopped.</strong> Find the stop button, the
            speed-down button and the safety key before you ever press start. Practise pressing
            stop with your eyes on the console. Clip the key's cord to your waistband so that if
            you drift backwards, the key pulls out and the belt stops.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the space.</strong> Keep the area behind the treadmill clear: no
            furniture, no walls close behind, no rugs, no pets. Your manual gives a minimum
            clearance; follow it. Good lighting and a fan help more than people expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Get on and off the same way every time.</strong> Stand on the side rails, hold
            the handrails, start the belt at its lowest speed, then step on. To finish, slow to the
            lowest speed, stop the belt completely, and only then step off. Our step-by-step guide
            to <a href="/how-to-get-on-and-off-a-treadmill-safely/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">getting on and off a treadmill safely</a> covers this in detail, and our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a> cover the rest of the setup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-hard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Hard Should It Feel?</h2>
<p>
            Speed charts are a poor guide for older walkers, because comfortable speeds vary so
            much with height, fitness, joints and balance. Effort is the better measure, and you
            can judge it two simple ways.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The talk test.</strong> At an easy effort you can chat freely, even sing. At a
            moderate effort, which is the target for most of this plan, your breathing is
            noticeably quicker but you can still speak in full sentences. If you can only manage a
            few words at a time, slow down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The 0 to 10 effort scale.</strong> Rate how hard the walk feels, where 0 is
            sitting in a chair and 10 is the hardest effort you could manage. This is often called
            RPE, for rating of perceived exertion.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Effort (0–10)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How it feels</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Talk test</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Used in this plan for</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1–2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Very easy, like strolling round a shop</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Could sing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warm-up and cool-down</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3–4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Easy to comfortable, breathing a little deeper</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Chatting freely</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weeks 1–2, recovery minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5–6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate, purposeful, slightly warm</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Full sentences, with pauses for breath</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Main part of weeks 3–6</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Somewhat hard</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Short phrases</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Only the brief "brisk" minutes in week 5–6 intervals</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8–10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hard to maximal</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A word or two</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not used</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>A cross-check: steps per minute.</strong> The CADENCE-Adults study, which
            measured 61 to 85-year-olds walking on a treadmill, found that about 100 steps per
            minute corresponded to moderate-intensity walking in absolute terms, and a 2023
            follow-up looking at heart rate and perceived effort put the best overall moderate
            threshold at about 105 steps per minute. Count your steps for 15 seconds and multiply
            by four. If you are well under 100, you are probably walking gently, which is fine in
            the first weeks. These are averages from a laboratory, not targets you must hit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-speed-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed for seniors</a> goes further into typical speeds by age and how to find your own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warm-up-cool-down" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warm-Up and Cool-Down</h2>
<p>
            Every session in this plan starts and ends with slow walking. The warm-up lets your
            heart rate rise gradually, warms the joints, and gives you a minute or two to settle
            into the rhythm of the belt before you ask anything of yourself. The cool-down lets
            your heart rate and blood pressure come down gradually, which matters because stopping
            suddenly after effort can leave some people light-headed.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Phase</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Time</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Speed</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Incline</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Effort (0–10)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warm-up, part 1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2–3 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lowest comfortable speed, often 0.8–1.5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1–2</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warm-up, part 2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Raise by 0.1–0.2 mph steps toward your session speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2–3</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cool-down, part 1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2–3 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Back to your warm-up speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Return to 0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cool-down, part 2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1–2 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slowest speed, then stop the belt fully</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Bring the incline back to zero before the cool-down, not after. Stepping off a stopped
            belt that is still tilted is awkward, and a level belt makes the last minutes feel
            steadier. After you step off, stand next to the treadmill holding a rail or a counter
            for a moment before walking away. If you feel dizzy, sit down.
          </p>
          <p>
            Gentle stretches for the calves and the fronts of the hips can follow, done standing
            beside a counter or chair. Our page on the <a href="/treadmill-warm-up-and-cool-down/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warm-up and cool-down</a> has more options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The 6-Week Plan</h2>
<p>
            The plan below assumes you can walk unaided around the house and the shops, and have
            your doctor's agreement if any of the conditions above apply. Times are for the main
            part of the session only; add the warm-up and cool-down on top, which adds about 8 to
            10 minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Speed ranges are deliberately wide. Use the one that produces the effort in the right
            column for you. A 78-year-old who reaches a moderate effort at 1.8 mph and a
            66-year-old who needs 2.8 mph for the same feeling are doing the same workout.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[640px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Week</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Days</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Main walk</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical speed</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Incline</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Effort (0–10) / talk test</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3, with a rest day between</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.0–2.0 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3–4, chatting freely</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12–15 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.2–2.2 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3–4, chatting freely</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3–4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15–20 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.5–2.5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–1%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4–5, full sentences</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3–4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 min (one day: gentle incline session)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.5–2.6 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–2%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5, full sentences</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20–25 min (one day: short intervals)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.6–2.8 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–3%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5–6, sentences with pauses</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4–5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">25–30 min (one incline, one interval day)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.8–3.0 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–3%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5–6, sentences with pauses</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Repeating a week is normal.</strong> If week 3 still feels like hard work at
            the end of the week, do week 3 again. Many people repeat at least one week, and some
            repeat several. The plan takes as long as it takes; there is no prize for finishing in
            six weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Splitting is allowed.</strong> Two 10-minute walks in a day count much the same
            as one 20-minute walk for most purposes, and they can be easier on the knees and hips.
            If 20 minutes in one go is too much, split it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where this leads.</strong> At the end of week 6 you are walking 25 to 30
            minutes four or five times a week, which is roughly 100 to 150 minutes, close to the
            150 minutes of moderate activity a week that the US Physical Activity Guidelines
            recommend for adults, older adults included. You do not need to go beyond 30 minutes a
            session to be doing well. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-should-seniors-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long seniors should walk on a treadmill</a> looks at session length in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sample-sessions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Three Sample Sessions</h2>
<p>
            From week 4 onwards, the plan uses three kinds of session. Most of your walks should be
            the easy steady session. The incline and interval sessions are for variety and a little
            extra challenge, once or twice a week at most. Times below include the warm-up and
            cool-down.
          </p>
          <h3>Session A: Easy steady walk (25 minutes)</h3>
          <p>
            The everyday session. One speed, flat or almost flat, at a pace where you could hold a
            conversation the whole way through.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
