              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10–19</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repeat 1 min brisk, 2 min easy, three more times</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">As above</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 / 6–7</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">19–24</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cool-down</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warm-up speed, then slowest</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1–2</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            A good week from week 6 on might be Session A on Monday and Friday, Session B on
            Wednesday, and Session C on Saturday, with balance and strength work on two of the
            other days. If the interval session ever leaves you more tired the next day than a
            normal walk, drop it for a week and use Session A instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="balance-strength" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Balance and Strength Add-Ons, Beside the Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Walking is good for the heart and for stamina, but on its own it does not do much for
            balance or leg strength, which are what keep people upright. The 2019 Cochrane review
            of exercise for preventing falls in older people living in the community, which pooled
            108 randomised trials, found that exercise programmes reduced the rate of falls by
            around 23%. The strongest evidence was for programmes based on balance and functional
            exercises; the reviewers were uncertain about the effect of walking on its own. The US
            Physical Activity Guidelines likewise recommend that older adults combine aerobic
            activity with muscle-strengthening and balance work.
          </p>
          <p>
            So this plan adds a short routine twice a week. <strong>Do it beside the treadmill,
            never on it.</strong> Stop the belt, remove the safety key, and stand next to a sturdy
            kitchen counter or the back of a heavy chair that will not slide. The handrail of a
            stopped treadmill is fine to touch for balance, but do not stand on the deck: belts can
            shift underfoot even when stopped, and the deck is a step up.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[600px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Exercise</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Start with</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Make it harder (later)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sit-to-stand</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">From a firm chair with arms, stand up fully, then sit back down slowly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5–8 reps, using hands</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10–12 reps, arms crossed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heel raises</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hold the counter, rise onto the balls of the feet, lower slowly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8–10 reps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 reps, lighter hand contact</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Side leg raise</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hold the counter, lift one leg out to the side with toes forward</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6–8 each side</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10–12 each side, slower</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Narrow stance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Feet together, hands hovering just above the counter</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10–20 seconds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30 seconds; then one foot half in front of the other</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">One-leg stand</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fingertips on the counter, lift one foot an inch</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5–10 seconds each side</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Up to 30 seconds, one fingertip</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wall push-up</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hands on a wall at chest height, lower chest toward it, push back</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6–8 reps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 reps, feet further back</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Move slowly, breathe normally rather than holding your breath, and keep a hand within
            reach of support for every balance exercise. If you have osteoporosis, a joint
            replacement or a condition that affects balance, a physical therapist can tailor this
            list; many of the programmes in the Cochrane review were supervised at the start.
          </p>
          <p>
            Classes such as tai chi are a good complement too. The same review found low-certainty
            evidence that tai chi reduces the rate of falls, and it is one of the activities the
            federal guidance gives as an example of multicomponent exercise for older adults.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-progress" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Progress</h2>
<p>
            Move to the next week only when the current one has felt comfortable. A useful rule:
            progress when you can finish every session of the week at the target effort, feel
            fine the next morning, and have had no new joint pain that lasted into the following
            day.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Change one thing at a time, in this order.</strong>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Time first.</strong> Add 2 to 5 minutes to the main walk. Longer at the
            same easy effort is the safest way to build fitness.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline second.</strong> Add 1% to the grade on one session a week. Do not
            go above about 3% in this plan; steeper walking is covered in our guide to <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk on</a> once you are ready.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed last.</strong> Add 0.1 to 0.2 mph. Speed changes your balance demands
            more than time or grade, so it comes last and in the smallest steps.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            <strong>Reduce your reliance on the handrails gradually.</strong> Light fingertip
            contact for balance is fine, and for some people it is the right long-term choice.
            Gripping hard to keep up with the belt, though, means the speed is too high. If you
            want to walk with less support, try letting go for 30 seconds at a time at a slower
            speed than usual, with your hands hovering just above the rails.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>After week 6.</strong> Keep the shape of the week, and progress slowly toward
            30 to 40 minutes on your easy days. When you want more variety, move on to the
            steady brisk walk and the gentle incline intervals in <a href="/treadmill-walking-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our six walking sessions</a>,
            keeping the speeds at the lower end of the ranges given there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-back-off" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Back Off, and When to Stop</h2>
<p>
            Knowing when to ease off is as important as knowing when to push on. Older bodies
            adapt well to exercise, but they recover more slowly, and a setback of a few weeks
            costs more than a cautious week does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stop straight away, and seek medical help, if you have any of these:</strong> chest pain, pressure or tightness; pain spreading to the arm, neck or jaw; sudden or
            severe breathlessness; a racing or irregular heartbeat; dizziness, light-headedness or
            feeling faint; confusion or blurred vision. Press stop or pull the safety key, <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>hold the
            rails</a>, and step off only when the belt has stopped. Sit down. Call 911 for chest pain
            or symptoms that do not settle quickly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Go back a week, or take a few days off, if:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>a knee, hip or ankle aches during the walk and is still sore the next day;</li>
            <li>you feel unusually tired the day after a session, or sleep badly;</li>
            <li>you have had a cold, a chest infection, or any illness, even a mild one;</li>
            <li>your medication has changed, especially blood-pressure or heart medication;</li>
            <li>you notice you are catching your toes, drifting toward the back of the belt, or
            needing to grip the rails harder than usual.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Mild muscle tiredness in the calves and thighs for a day or so is normal when you
            start. Sharp pain, swelling, or pain that changes the way you walk is not. If you have
            arthritis, some discomfort can be expected, and a physical therapist can help you tell
            the difference; our guide to <a href="/treadmill-walking-with-arthritis/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking with arthritis</a> covers how to adjust the plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hot days and early mornings.</strong> If the room is warm, use a fan and drink
            water, and expect the same speed to feel harder. Some people feel stiffer first thing
            in the morning; walking later in the day is perfectly fine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-right-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Does Your Treadmill Suit the Plan?</h2>
<p>
            This plan needs very little from a treadmill, but a few features make it easier and
            safer for older walkers.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A low starting speed.</strong> Many machines start at 0.5 mph, and some <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pads</a> at similar speeds. A belt that jumps straight to 1.5 mph makes the first
            weeks harder.</li>
            <li><strong>Small speed steps.</strong> Buttons that change speed by 0.1 mph let you
            follow the progression closely.</li>
            <li><strong>Full-length handrails.</strong> Rails that run along both sides of the deck
            give support for the whole walk and for getting on and off. Our roundup of <a href="/best-treadmills-with-handrails/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with long handrails</a> covers machines built this way.</li>
            <li><strong>A low step-up height.</strong> A lower deck is easier to get onto,
            especially with stiff hips or knees.</li>
            <li><strong>A powered incline</strong> is useful for Session B but not essential. If
            your machine is flat, do Session A or C in its place.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are still choosing a machine, our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for seniors</a> compares models on these points. If you are not sure a treadmill is right for you at
            all, our honest look at <a href="/is-a-treadmill-good-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a treadmill is good for seniors</a> sets out the benefits, the fall risk and the alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Start with 10 minutes of easy, flat walking three times a week, and build to 25
            to 30 minutes four or five times a week over six weeks or more.</strong> Let the talk
            test set the speed, add time before incline and incline before speed, and keep the
            incline at 3% or below.
          </p>
          <p>
            Add a short balance and strength routine twice a week, done beside the treadmill with
            the belt stopped, because that is the part that does most for steadiness. Clip on the
            safety key, repeat weeks whenever you need to, and treat chest pain, dizziness or new
            joint pain as reasons to stop and get advice, not to push through.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Older Walkers Make on the Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most setbacks in the first weeks come from doing a little too much, too soon, or from small safety habits being skipped.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Starting at the speed you walk outdoors</h3>
              <p>Studies of older adults find they tend to walk more slowly and with shorter steps on a treadmill than over ground until they are used to it. Start below your usual outdoor pace, at a speed that feels almost too easy, and let the speed rise over weeks rather than in the first session.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Changing speed, incline and time all at once</h3>
              <p>Adding five minutes, 2% grade and half a mile per hour in the same week is the quickest route to sore knees or a wobble on the belt. Change one thing at a time: time first, then a little incline, then a little speed.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping the safety key</h3>
              <p>The clip-on key is the one feature that stops the belt if you drift back or lose your footing. It only works if it is attached to you. Clip it to your waistband before you start the belt, every time, including short sessions.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Doing balance exercises on the belt</h3>
              <p>Standing on one leg or with feet together on a treadmill deck, even a stopped one, puts you on a raised, narrow surface that can move. Do balance and strength work on the floor beside a sturdy counter or chair, with the belt stopped and the key removed.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The takeaway</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Start with 10 minutes of easy, flat walking three days a week</strong> and build to 25 to 30 minutes four or five days a week, letting the talk test set the
            speed. Add time first, then a gentle incline of up to 3%, then a little speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do a short balance and strength routine twice a week beside the treadmill, clip on the
            safety key every session, and stop for chest pain, dizziness or new joint pain. If you
            are choosing a machine, see the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best treadmills for seniors</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

