        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is a good treadmill workout for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most older adults, a good workout is a few minutes of slow warm-up walking, 10 to 30 minutes at an effort where you can still talk in full sentences, and a few minutes of slow cool-down, three to five days a week. Start flat and slow, often 1.0 to 2.0 mph, and build time before adding a gentle incline of 1 to 3% or a little speed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should a 70-year-old walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Begin with about 10 minutes of main walking plus warm-up and cool-down, and build toward 20 to 30 minutes over several weeks. Four or five 30-minute walks add up to the 150 minutes of moderate activity a week the US guidelines recommend, but any amount helps, and two 10-minute walks are a reasonable substitute for one 20-minute walk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should a senior walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no single right speed. Many older adults start between 1.0 and 2.0 mph and settle somewhere around 1.5 to 3.0 mph for their main walk. Use the talk test: for most of the session you should be breathing a little harder but still able to talk in full sentences. Change speed in 0.1 to 0.2 mph steps.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should seniors use incline on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A gentle incline of 1 to 3% is a good way to add effort without walking faster, which many older walkers find steadier. Build up to it after two or three weeks of flat walking, use it on one or two sessions a week, and check with a physical therapist first if you have knee or hip arthritis or a recent joint replacement.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it OK for seniors to hold the treadmill handrails?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Light contact for balance is what the rails are for, and for some people it is the right long-term choice. What to avoid is gripping hard to keep up with the belt or leaning on the rails, which means the speed or incline is too high. Slow down until a light touch is enough.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many days a week should seniors do treadmill workouts?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Three days a week is a good start, with a rest day between sessions. Build to four or five days as the walks feel comfortable, and add balance and strength exercises on two days a week. Rest or go back a week if you feel unusually tired or have joint pain that lasts into the next day.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

